---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout title="Research" description="What we tested. What we didn't.">
  <div class="report-page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>What we’ve tested</h1>
      <p class="page-note">What we tested. What we didn't.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="report-wrap">
      <article class="sheet report" aria-labelledby="report-title">
        <div class="sheet-content">
          <h2 class="report-heading" id="report-title">
            # Research
          </h2>
          <p class="applies">
            Applies when someone asks how we know any of this works.
          </p>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="style-guide-pilot">
            <h3 id="style-guide-pilot">
              Do good samples need a written style guide?
            </h3>
            <p>
              <strong>What this shows:</strong> no clear overall benefit from
              adding this particular guide in this small pilot. The guide was
              preferred more often on voice alone, but not overall. Real writing
              stayed the practical default, and a guide stayed optional. Milton
              now keeps the finals you accept as examples, beside a cover sheet
              you write, and the AI you already use does the writing.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Working conclusion:</strong> capable models may be able to
              follow a person's style from good examples, without an elaborate
              written guide. That is our hypothesis. We have not measured it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>What we measured:</strong> whether adding one particular
              guide to the same samples made first drafts better.
            </p>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Samples:</strong> three Paul Graham essays.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Tasks:</strong> 10 fictional briefs, made up of 3
                essays, 3 posts, 2 emails, and 2 DMs.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Drafts:</strong> 20 first drafts from GPT-6.1-Sol at
                medium reasoning, two for each brief.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Judging:</strong> Claude Opus 5.5 at high effort,
                working independently of the writer, compared each pair twice,
                once in each candidate order. It was not told which draft had
                the guide.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Controls:</strong> within each pair, the samples, model,
                and brief were the same. Only the guide differed.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Guide:</strong> the latest one supplied from Spiral,
                another writing tool. It was fixed before the drafts were
                written and not revised after we saw them.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <table class="report-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Outcome, out of 10 tasks</th>
                  <th scope="col">Overall</th>
                  <th scope="col">Voice only</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Samples alone preferred</th>
                  <td>5</td>
                  <td>3</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Samples plus guide preferred</th>
                  <td>4</td>
                  <td>5</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Inconclusive across orders</th>
                  <td>1</td>
                  <td>2</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>
              A task counts for one side only when both orders chose that side.
              Mixed results count as inconclusive. All 20 drafts met their
              requested word ranges.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>What this doesn't show:</strong>
            </p>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>
                What people prefer. The judge was an AI, not a person, and we
                measured neither human preference nor editing time.
              </li>
              <li>
                Anything broader than this setup: one familiar author, one
                writer model, one guide, and one judge.
              </li>
              <li>
                How much samples help. Every draft had samples, so nothing was
                compared with drafts written without them.
              </li>
              <li>
                That all GPT or Claude models work equally well, or that Milton
                achieves any percentage of Spiral's quality.
              </li>
              <li>
                How a real person writes email or DMs. Those drafts carry a
                style over from essays, not from real correspondence.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Full notes:{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/zoneofdanger/milton/blob/main/docs/research/pg-style-guide-ablation-2026-11-05.md">
                Method and results
              </a>{" "}
              and{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/zoneofdanger/milton/blob/main/docs/research/pg-style-guide-ablation-drafts-2026-11-05.md">
                All briefs and drafts
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="no-model">
            <h3 id="no-model">Why Milton doesn't rewrite your writing</h3>
            <p>
              Milton stores your memos and cover sheet exactly as your AI sends
              them. Reading them back is a database query, with no model call.
              Milton itself never rewrites what you saved. Your AI does the
              writing, with its own model. Milton keeps the file.
            </p>
            <p>
              We tested adding a model to how Milton picks examples. See below.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="sample-ranking">
            <h3 id="sample-ranking">How Milton picks examples</h3>
            <p>
              When we ran these tests, Milton served eight samples per report,
              ranked by who they were for and what they were for, then by
              matching words, then by date. It now ranks your memos by kind,
              medium, who and what they were for, whether the final is your own
              words, then matching words and date, and serves as many as fit in
              a fixed budget, with at most three drafts you steered.
            </p>
            <p>
              We tested that against two alternatives: the newest eight, and
              Google's embedding model, which matches by meaning. We ran it
              twice, on fictional writers, with drafts scored blind:
            </p>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Round 1:</strong> one writer, 150 samples, 20 drafted
                situations.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Round 2:</strong> three writers, 1,500 samples, 120
                drafted situations.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <table class="report-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Round 2</th>
                  <th scope="col">Found the right samples, 0 to 1</th>
                  <th scope="col">Draft score, out of 5</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Newest eight</th>
                  <td>0.32</td>
                  <td>3.77</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Milton's ranking</th>
                  <td>0.70</td>
                  <td>3.72</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Embeddings plus Milton's ranking</th>
                  <td>0.73</td>
                  <td>3.68</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>
              Embeddings found slightly better matches and wrote no better
              drafts. They would also send your samples to a third party on
              every read. We left them out.
            </p>
            <p>
              The result we didn't expect:{" "}
              <strong>
                finding better samples did not produce better drafts.
              </strong>{" "}
              Our fictional writers wrote the same way to everyone, so any eight
              of their samples sounded alike. Real people write differently to a
              client than to a friend, which is where picking the right sample
              should matter. That test needs real writing, and it is next.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="sample-count">
            <h3 id="sample-count">How many samples</h3>
            <p>
              Real writing teaches an AI more than nothing. In one study, five
              real emails roughly doubled how often a model's email could be
              traced to the person it was imitating, from 29% to 60%, and two
              emails did about as well as ten. That is why setup asks for about
              five pieces you wrote and sent.
            </p>
            <p>
              In our earlier ranking tests, cutting from eight samples to four
              made drafts clearly worse, 3.21 against 3.65 out of 5. Milton now
              serves as many matching examples as fit in a fixed budget, not a
              fixed count.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="untested">
            <h3 id="untested">What we haven't tested yet</h3>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>Drafts for real people, judged by those people.</li>
              <li>
                How well ChatGPT, Codex, and other clients beyond Claude Code
                retrieve examples from Milton and write from them. The
                style-guide pilot was an offline prompt test: the samples were
                placed in the prompt, and no client fetched them from Milton.
              </li>
              <li>Whether one short email is enough.</li>
              <li>
                Whether a cover sheet or tweaks add anything beyond pasted
                writing. The style-guide pilot tested one fixed guide, not
                those.
              </li>
              <li>Whether you can pick out your own writing, blind.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section class="report-section" aria-labelledby="test-method">
            <h3 id="test-method">How the earlier ranking tests were run</h3>
            <ul class="instruction-list">
              <li>
                The fictional writers and their samples were written by a model.
              </li>
              <li>
                Drafts were scored blind by a second model, twice each, against
                a description of the writer.
              </li>
              <li>
                No person has scored them yet. People are generous judges of
                their own voice: in one study, they rated drafts they had edited
                as just as much their own as text they wrote alone, though the
                drafts were measurably closer to the model's. So a person's
                score needs to be blind too.
              </li>
              <li>We set the pass mark before each run.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Full notes:{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/zoneofdanger/milton/blob/main/docs/research/sample-ranking-2026.md">
                Sample ranking
              </a>{" "}
              and{" "}
              <a href="https://github.com/zoneofdanger/milton/blob/main/docs/research/writing-evidence-2026.md">
                What the evidence supports
              </a>
              .
            </p>
            <p>
              Sources: Wang et al. (2025),{" "}
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.14543v1">
                Catch Me If You Can? Not Yet
              </a>
              ; Baumler et al. (2026),{" "}
              <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.24444">
                Can You Make It Sound Like You?
              </a>
              .
            </p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
